    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters May 9 with a high-ceiling lineup led by **Corbin Carroll** (79.02) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91), our two most potent offensive options. **Corbin Carroll** benefits from a plus-matchup against Holmes, where his elite speed remains a major factor despite a slight de-rating for the pitcher\u2019s ability to hold runners. **Tyler Soderstrom** is our standout play of the day, maximizing a massive +16% park factor and a favorable look at Baz to secure his elite status. **Josh Naylor** also anchors the middle order with a 71.44 score; he is poised to capitalize on a weak 5.28 xERA from Kay, further bolstered by wind patterns blowing out to provide an additional offensive cushion.\n\nWe are navigating a few tactical constraints in today\u2019s selection. **Mickey Moniak** (66.49) remains a strong start despite Nola\u2019s presence, as his platoon advantage and batting order stability outweigh the risk of the elite catcher\u2019s pop time. The most difficult bench decision involved **Austin Riley**, whose 49.30 score just missed the cut. While **Austin Riley** offers pedigree, the optimizer favored the higher current efficiency of our middle-infield starters like **Jose Caballero** (56.90) and **Cole Young** (56.63) due to their specific platoon advantages and the favorable wind conditions assisting **Cole Young**. Meanwhile, **Travis Bazzana** fell below the 40.0 efficiency floor, compounded by a 45% rain risk that made him an easy exclusion.\n\nManagers should monitor the lineup status for **Dalton Rushing** and **Alex Freeland**, as both currently rely on projected spots; should **Dalton Rushing** slide lower than the #7 hole, his marginal score could drop into the sub-floor range. While **Gabriel Moreno** and **Vinnie Pasquantino** are set in the lineup, they face stiff challenges from Holmes and Smith respectively. We are prioritizing speed and volume today, banking on the high-floor production of **James Wood** (63.92) to keep us competitive in stolen bases while our core bats target the weaker arms in the rotation.",
